      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and plays a central role in Earth’s physical and biological systems. Here’s a concise overview:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical Formula** | H₂O – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular Structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° bond angle) giving it a polar character. |\n| **States of Matter** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing) and 100 °C (boiling) under standard atmospheric pressure. |\n| **Key Physical Properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs a lot of heat before temperature rises, moderating climates. <br>• **High surface tension** – allows insects to walk on it and creates droplets. <br>• **Excellent solvent** – dissolves many substances, enabling biochemical reactions. <br>• **Transparency** – allows light penetration, supporting aquatic life. |\n| **Biological Importance** | • **Solvent for cells** – transports nutrients, waste, and signals. <br>• **Regulates temperature** – through sweating, respiration, and circulation. <br>• **Chemical reactions** – participates in metabolism (e.g., hydrolysis, photosynthesis). |\n| **Geological & Environmental Role** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff. <br>• **Erosion & weathering** – shapes landscapes. <br>• **Climate regulation** – stores and transports heat. |\n| **Human Uses** | • **Drinking & sanitation** – essential for health. <br>• **Industrial** – cooling, chemical processing, energy generation. <br>• **Agriculture** – irrigation. <br>• **Recreation** – swimming, sports. |\n| **Unique Features** | • **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float and insulate aquatic life in winter. <br>• **High cohesion & adhesion** – leads to capillary action, enabling water to move up plant stems. |\n\nIn short, water is a polar, tri‑atomic molecule that exists naturally in three states, possesses extraordinary physical properties, and is indispensable for life, ecosystems, and human civilization."
